{"id":"z-test-one-sample","t":"One-sample z-test with known sigma","tags":["Probability and Statistics"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"z_test","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"When the population standard deviation $\\sigma$ is **known** (for example, a sensor's documented noise level), you can test whether the mean equals $\\mu_0$ with\n\n$$z = \\frac{\\bar x - \\mu_0}{\\sigma / \\sqrt n}$$\n\nUnder $H_0$, $z \\sim \\mathcal N(0, 1)$. The p-value depends on the alternative hypothesis, with $\\Phi$ the standard normal cdf:\n\n| `alternative` | $H_1$ | p-value |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `'two-sided'` | $\\mu \\ne \\mu_0$ | $2\\,(1 - \\Phi(\\lvert z\\rvert))$ |\n| `'greater'` | $\\mu > \\mu_0$ | $1 - \\Phi(z)$ |\n| `'less'` | $\\mu < \\mu_0$ | $\\Phi(z)$ |\n\nWrite `z_test(x, mu0, sigma, alternative='two-sided')` that returns the tuple `(z, p)` as two Python floats.\n\n## Requirements\n- `x`: non-empty 1-D float array. `sigma` $> 0$.\n- Compute tail probabilities with `math.erfc`, using $1 - \\Phi(z) = \\tfrac12\\,\\mathrm{erfc}(z/\\sqrt2)$, so small p-values keep their precision.","starter":"import math\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef z_test(x, mu0, sigma, alternative='two-sided'):\n    \"\"\"Return (z, p_value) as floats.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["The standard error of the mean is $\\sigma/\\sqrt n$. The statistic is how many standard errors $\\bar x$ sits from $\\mu_0$.","Write one helper for the upper tail, $P(Z > v) = \\tfrac12\\,\\mathrm{erfc}(v/\\sqrt2)$. Every alternative is that helper applied to $|z|$, $z$ or $-z$.","The two-sided p-value doubles the tail beyond $|z|$. For `\"less\"`, use the symmetry $\\Phi(z) = P(Z > -z)$."],"sol":"import math\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef z_test(x, mu0, sigma, alternative='two-sided'):\n    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=float)\n    z = float((x.mean() - mu0) / (sigma / math.sqrt(len(x))))\n    upper = lambda v: 0.5 * math.erfc(v / math.sqrt(2))     # P(Z > v)\n    if alternative == 'two-sided':\n        p = 2 * upper(abs(z))\n    elif alternative == 'greater':\n        p = upper(z)\n    else:\n        p = upper(-z)                                       # P(Z < z) = P(Z > -z)\n    return z, p\n","ex_md":"## What the p-value is\nIt's the probability, **assuming $H_0$ is true**, of a statistic at least as extreme as the one observed, where \"extreme\" is defined by $H_1$. It is not the probability that $H_0$ is true. A small p-value says the data would be surprising under $H_0$.\n\n## Why erfc\nFor $z = 17.3$ (test 5), $1 - \\Phi(z)$ computed as `1 - cdf` is exactly 0, while `erfc` returns the true value, about $10^{-67}$. The answer matters when you rank thousands of features by p-value or apply a multiple-testing correction.\n\n## When to use z versus t\nOnly use z when $\\sigma$ is genuinely known, or when $n$ is large enough that the sample $s$ is a near-exact stand-in. With an estimated $s$ and small $n$, use the t-test: its heavier tails account for the uncertainty in $s$.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(n)$ for the mean.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Dividing by $\\sigma$ instead of $\\sigma / \\sqrt n$.\n- Doubling the one-sided p-value for the wrong tail. With `'greater'` and a negative $z$, the p-value is above 0.5. It doesn't flip sign.\n- Calling a one-sided test after looking at which direction the data went. That doubles the real false-positive rate.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- How many samples do you need to detect a shift of $\\delta$ with power $1 - \\beta$ at level $\\alpha$? ($n = \\left(\\frac{(z_{1-\\alpha/2} + z_{1-\\beta})\\,\\sigma}{\\delta}\\right)^2$.)\n- What's the link between this test and a 95% confidence interval for $\\mu$? (Reject at 5% exactly when $\\mu_0$ is outside $\\bar x \\pm 1.96\\,\\sigma/\\sqrt n$.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([5.2, 4.8, 5.6, 5.4])\n5.0\n0.5","out":"(1.0, 0.3173105078629141)"},{"in":"array([ 98. , 102. , 101. ,  99.5, 103. ])\n100.0\n2.0\n'greater'","out":"(0.7826237921249296, 0.21692403288321868)"}],"lessons":[{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ml-maths-course/inference/the-sample-mean/","t":"The Sample Mean","c":"ML Maths"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ml-maths-course/statistics-and-experiments/hypothesis-testing-z-t-and-p-values/","t":"Hypothesis testing: z, t and p-values","c":"ML Maths"}],"related":["normal-distribution","t-test-one-sample","one-sample-t-statistic"],"ntests":7,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [5.2, 4.8, 5.6, 5.4], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 5.0, 0.5], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [1.0, 0.3173105078629141]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [98.0, 102.0, 101.0, 99.5, 103.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 100.0, 2.0, \"greater\"], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [0.7826237921249296, 0.21692403288321868]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [98.0, 102.0, 101.0, 99.5, 103.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 100.0, 2.0, \"less\"], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [0.7826237921249296, 0.7830759671167813]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [3.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 3.0, 1.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [0.0, 1.0]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [10.0, 10.0, 10.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.0, 1.0, \"two-sided\"], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [17.32050807568877, 3.2943623833142004e-67]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [-0.0700247693240037, 1.294078692032213, 0.7158574806463202, -0.3181637087164065, 0.9721408015696635, -1.150049505885249, 0.8934846357693562, -0.26182972354409545, 0.931453096950567, 0.7390906518766012, -0.46724880591175005, 0.8283687488069336, 0.6419276735141769, -0.3461430903975194, 2.301150555213166, 1.10195993578643, -0.8820652577744168, -0.6879819159784637, 0.6168316854272728, 0.6120442520839395, -0.4268560483413962, 1.5158778403220516, 0.3970171160950695, -0.5626833404289537, -0.07459771012863509, 0.12120660563225996, -1.2533105934432882, -0.5322121471805925, -1.509912000528707, 0.006443826852684742, 0.9126395357012109, -0.9858506769982023, 0.6220862599757191, -0.8826669740084903, 0.13637429819796446, -0.21616602108150512, -0.9121831966594529, -0.24909849878388163, -0.9254692506871665, 0.5935107219230473, 0.34617552533742385, 1.499963415268209,